title: Hemp
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki

Grows fast, needs little water, and needs few pesticides. A hectare of it can take in 8 to 15 tonnes of CO2.

The fiber is strong and barely stretches. Upholsterers used hemp webbing and twine under cushions for a long time before synthetics.

The [[Oka Chair]] uses hemp cord the old way, as the suspension under the seat, and leaves it showing. The [[Peel Chair]] uses it the new way, mixed into [[PLA]] and made into foam.

In the USA the 1937 Marijuana Tax Act mostly ended hemp farming. It is coming back.

!! Where the Oka Chair's cord comes from

Justin Beitzel first sourced hemp from a farm in western Maryland and tried making the rope himself: retting, hackling, weaving. A few feet took so long that the 300 feet a seat needs was out of reach. He then found a rope maker in North Carolina working with domestic hemp fiber. The plan was to send their own fiber there to be made into rope as orders grew, and eventually to buy machines.

Three years later that rope maker has closed. Today the rope is made by an American company, but the fiber comes from France.

So this is where the chair's [[BioRegion]] breaks. The wood and the wool are local; the hemp is not. The U.S. has almost no fiber processing left, and a small maker can't rebuild it alone.

//Source: correspondence with Justin Beitzel, October 2026.//
